Change: cmd/launcher/skills.go sweepSkillDirs collects every rename/RemoveAll error and goes on to the next entry, returning errors.Join(errs...). An entry gone before its rename (taken by a second sweep) is still skipped silently. materializeSkills already appends the sweep error to its own join, and setupSkills turns the newlines into '; ', so every failed leftover lands in the one existing WARN line. The doc comment says so. No new message text.

Test: TestSweepRemovesEveryLeftoverItCan (cmd/launcher/skills_test.go, new section k164). It runs through setupSkills with leftovers unreagent-.tmp-a, -b and -c, where a and c are read-only with a file inside. It asserts that b is removed, only the two held leftovers remain, the set is in place, there is one WARN line, and 'permission denied' appears twice. Without the fix it fails three ways: b is still there, this run's own work dir is left too, and the warning names 1 failure. Skips on Windows and as root, the same as the existing read-only tests.

Not covered: the rename-fails branch. On Linux a rename in the same parent cannot be made to fail for one entry without root, so only the RemoveAll branch is exercised. A Windows AV-held file most likely fails at that rename, so the Windows path is compiled (GOOS=windows vet, make windows) but unverified.

